      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027ve tested the `PR_SET_PDEATHSIG` bit in a stand-alone app (on my Linux machine):\n```\nfn main() {\n    let arg \u003d env::args().skip(1).next().unwrap_or(\"0\".to_string());\n    let mut command \u003d Command::new(\"sleep\");\n    if arg.parse::\u003cusize\u003e().unwrap() !\u003d 0 {\n        unsafe {\n            command.pre_exec(|| match prctl(PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, SIGKILL) {\n                0 \u003d\u003e Ok(()),\n                _ \u003d\u003e Err(io::Error::last_os_error()),\n            });\n        }\n    }\n    let _ \u003d command.arg(\"32\").output().unwrap();\n}\n```\nand it seems to be WAI for me i.e. `kill $(pidof test_prctl)` kills the `sleep` process when running `cargo run 1` but doesn\u0027t if running `cargo run`.\n\n\u003e `virtmgr` became a zombie process\n\nI guess that\u0027s the issue; can\u0027t receive a `PDEATHSIG` if there\u0027s no \"parent death\"!\n\nBut how is `virtmgr` managing to survive `SIGKILL`...?\n\nI\u0027ll cherry-pick this commit and investigate.",
